        "description": "<img src='https://i.cbc.ca/ais/49b881a3-798d-4129-a816-6779c04b18e9,1773104509487/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%280%2C442%2C4096%2C2304%29%3BResize%3D%28620%29' alt='A group of people in uniform pose on the deck of a naval ship with large bales containing seized cocaine. ' width='620' height='349' title='On the deck of the HMCS Yellowknife in 2024, before lethal strikes began, Canadian sailors and US Coast Guard enforcement officers pose together with 1400kgs of cocaine seized by the ship as part of that year&apos;s Op Caribbe deployment.'/><p>HMCS Yellowknife has just ended its deployment in a U.S.-led anti-drug-smuggling mission in the Caribbean, where American airstrikes on suspected drug boats have killed over 150 people. Canadian officials say the Canadian Armed Forces and Canadian Coast Guard aren't involved in those strikes, and will ensure that intelligence about boats they detect and track is provided to the U.S. only on condition it not be shared with naval forces",
